    {
      "id": "eb11c1-3:definition-1",
      "type": "definition",
      "lo": "eb11c1-3:objective-1",
      "source_page": 14,
      "quote": "Dividing both the numerator and denominator of a rational expression by their common factor is called reducing.",
      "claim": "Dividing both the numerator and denominator of a rational expression by their common factor is called reducing.",
      "provenance": {
        "by": "runbook/book-record.workflow.js ext-v5, sonnet, 2026-10-21",
        "method": "page image"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "eb11c1-3:definition-2",
      "type": "definition",
      "lo": "eb11c1-3:objective-1",
      "source_page": 14,
      "quote": "A rational expression that cannot be reduced further is called an irreducible rational expression.",
      "claim": "A rational expression that cannot be reduced further is called an irreducible rational expression.",
      "provenance": {
        "by": "runbook/book-record.workflow.js ext-v5, sonnet, 2026-10-21",
        "method": "page image"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "eb11c1-3:example-1",
      "type": "worked_example",
      "lo": "eb11c1-3:objective-1",
      "source_page": 14,
      "quote": "Example: Reducing (x − 1)(x − 3) / x(x − 3) gives",
      "stem": "Reducing $\\frac{(x-1)(x-3)}{x(x-3)}$",
      "solution": [
        "$\\frac{(x-1)(x-3)}{x(x-3)}=\\frac{(x-1)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}=\\frac{x-1}{x}$"
      ],
      "answer": "$\\frac{x-1}{x}$",
      "solution_provenance": "book_worked",
      "notes": "Side note printed: 'Irreducible rational expression since it cannot be simplified further.' The example is unnumbered, inside the Point! box.",
      "provenance": {
        "by": "runbook/book-record.workflow.js ext-v5, sonnet, 2026-10-21",
        "method": "page image"
      }
    },
